d reference to `rem:ff-realization`, whose own three steps are audited at KC.21--KC.23, all `MISSING` on the same `FFF` input; this row records that Question 2 now leans on that remark, so deleting the remark could not silently orphan the question.  **PERMANENTLY MISSING BY DESIGN, recorded 2026-08-17 so that no later lane re-scopes it.**  The printed sentence is a forward pointer to another part of the manuscript, not an inference: it asserts that a route exists elsewhere, and there is no proposition here for a declaration to establish.  Its mathematical content, insofar as it has any, is KC.21's, and that is blocked where KC.21 is blocked.  Nothing can move this row except deleting the sentence  **THE ROUTE IS NOW BUILT, 2026-08-19; grade unchanged and the row is still permanently missing for the reason above.**  The forward pointer now points at something formal: `Sofic/SmallCancellationKazhdanEnvelope.lean` builds the small-cancellation route over the same group as a typed interface.  `HyperbolicKazhdanPartner` is the torsion-free hyperbolic Kazhdan group `H`; `SmallCancellationQuotient U H` is the output clause list of the step over the relatively hyperbolic pair `U * H` rel `U` — the peripheral factor `U` still embedded, the Kazhdan partner surjective, finite presentation and torsion-freeness preserved (Osin, Theorem 2.4.5).  **Property (T) of the envelope is derived, not stored:** `SmallCancellationQuotient.kazhdan` obtains it from the partner through `HasKazhdanPropertyT.of_surjective`, the discipline `RelativeCommonQuotientData.toDefectRoutingData` already follows, so the route postulates three clauses and derives the fourth.  `liftMap`/`liftMap_surjective` make the quotient literally a quotient of the free product, and `coprod_finitelyPresented` records that the source is itself finitely presented.  `universalKazhdanGroup` is the whole chain, host plus partner plus quotient, landing on `KC.21`.  Relative hyperbolicity is deliberately absent as a field, for the reason acylindrical hyperbolicity is absent from `RelativeCommonQuotientData`: this library has no definition of the notion and a field naming it would certify nothing.  **The measurement this makes possible:** `NormalGeneratorEnvelope` records Chatterji-Kassabov Theorem 3 (arXiv:2601.22907, established from source at `research/chatterji-kassabov-normal-generator-embedding.md`), which already embeds every finitely presented group into a finitely presented property-(T) group normally generated by a prescribed element, and `NormalGeneratorEnvelope.toKazhdanEnvelope` shows it lacks exactly one clause — torsion-freeness of the envelope.  So what Osin's Theorem 2.4.5 supplies in `\cite[\S2]{FFF}` is neither the embedding nor property (T) but the torsion clause alone.  Nothing inhabits any of the three structures, and the row does not move  **INTERFACE STRENGTHENED 2026-08-19.**  `Algebra/HyperbolicGroup.lean` now states Gromov hyperbolicity (the four-point condition on the word metric of a finite symmetric generating set), so `HyperbolicKazhdanPartner` carries `hyperbolic` and `Infinite` as fields rather than recording them in prose, and `HyperbolicKazhdanPartner.not_isAmenable` derives nonelementarity from those two through `infinite_kazhdan_not_isAmenable`.  The structure is now strictly harder to inhabit: the trivial group satisfies every clause the interface asserted before this change and satisfies it no longer.  The row is unaffected -- nothing inhabits the structure, and the grade stays MISSING/literature-input on FFF |
